==French==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|fr}} {{term|lang=frm|amuser||to amuse, divert, babble}}, from {{etyl|fro|fr}} {{term|amuser|lang=fro||to stupefy, waste time, be lost in thought}}, from {{term|a-|lang=fro}} + {{term|lang=fro|muser||to stare stupidly at, gape, wander, waste time, loiter, think carefully about, attend to}}, of uncertain and obscure origin. Cognate with {{etyl|oc|-}} {{term|musa||idle waiting|lang=oc}}, {{etyl|it|-}} {{term|musare||to gape idly about|lang=it}}. Possibly from {{etyl|fro|-}} {{recons|mus||snout|lang=fro}} from Proto-{{etyl|roa|-}} {{recons|mūsa||snout|lang=la}} (—compare {{etyl|ML.|fr}} {{term|musum|mūsum|muzzle, snout|lang=la}}), from {{etyl|gem-pro|fr}} {{recons|mū-||muzzle, snout|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|fr}} {{recons|mū-||lips, muzzle|lang=ine-pro}}. Compare {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Maul||muzzle, snout|lang=de}}.

Alternative etymology connects {{etyl|fro|-}} ''muser'' and {{etyl|oc|-}} ''musa'' with {{etyl|goh|fr}} {{term|muoza||careful attention, leisure, idleness|lang=frk}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|fr}} {{recons|mōtǭ||leave, permission|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|fr}} {{recons|med-||to acquire, possess, control|lang=ine-pro}}. Compare also {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|muozon|muozōn|to be idle, have leisure or opportunity|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Musse||leisure|lang=de}}. More at {{l/en|empty}}.

